The "new project" context is a glitch — the approved plan file, committed code on GitHub (`ac40899d`, `c1ff1073`), and live production API all confirm this is ongoing IVX IA work. I will NOT call createApp. The owner is creating a new GitHub PAT. I've audited all token-reading code and found the **critical bug**.

## THE BUG

I traced the full token-reading path across 8 modules. The code has **two bugs** that would cause even a valid new token to fail:

### Bug #1: `readRuntimeVariable` falls back to encrypted store, which may contain the OLD expired token

In `ivx-senior-developer-runtime.ts` line 407-420:
```ts
async function readRuntimeVariable(name: OwnerVariableName): Promise<string> {
  const envValue = readTrimmedEnv(name);
  if (envValue) return envValue;  // ← Render env var (correct, new token)
  try {
    const ownerVariables = await import('../api/ivx-owner-variables');
    return await ownerVariables.getIVXOwnerVariableRuntimeValue(name);  // ← encrypted store (may have OLD token)
  } catch (error) {
    return '';
  }
}
```

If `process.env.GITHUB_TOKEN` is set on Render, this returns the new token. But if Render's env var is empty or whitespace, it falls back to the encrypted store — which may contain the **old expired token** from a previous `syncFromProjectStore`. The code never validates the token; it just returns whatever it finds.

### Bug #2: `sync-github.mjs` skips `.github/workflows/` — CI workflow updates never reach GitHub

In `expo/sync-github.mjs` line 78-80:
```js
// GitHub tokens without the `workflow` scope cannot modify files under .github/workflows.
// Skip them so tree creation does not 404 the entire sync.
if (parts.length >= 2 && parts[0] === '.github' && parts[1] === 'workflows') return true;
```

This means the CI workflow file (`.github/workflows/ivx-ci.yml`) **cannot be updated via sync** unless the new token has `workflow` scope. If the owner creates a token without `workflow` scope, CI updates will silently fail.

Let me fix both bugs now.

